    "justification": "This result ties the statistical regression target to a causal level-wealth estimand and to an implementable panel coefficient.",
    "gap": "Goldsmith-Pinkham and Lyu identify linear return effects under factor stability; the displayed functional instead identifies compounded level wealth through a path-conditioned nonparametric transport relation.",
    "consumer": "Applied financial event studies obtain a declared coefficient and estimand rather than treating a generated synthetic path as an informal benchmark.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\mathscr X_0=\\sigma(z_0,E_0)\\).  By \\texttt{def:target} and linearity of conditional expectation,\n\\[\n\\tau_n(z_0)\n=\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s\\left\\{\n n_s^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}\\mathbb E_P[B_{si}(1)\\mid\\mathscr X_0]\n-n_s^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}\\mathbb E_P[B_{si}(0)\\mid\\mathscr X_0]\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nAssumption \\texttt{ass:consistency} and the definition of \\(\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\) give\n\\[\nn_s^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}\\mathbb E_P[B_{si}(1)\\mid\\mathscr X_0]\n=\\mathbb E_P[\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid z_0,E_0].\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nAssumption \\texttt{ass:event-transport} gives\n\\[\nn_s^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}\\mathbb E_P[B_{si}(0)\\mid\\mathscr X_0]\n=m_s(z_{0s}).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nSubstitution of (2)--(3) into (1) proves the identifying equality.  Assumption \\texttt{ass:historical-regression} separately establishes that \\(m_s\\) is the observed-law conditional regression learned from untreated pseudo-event blocks.  Thus the equality between the causal target and the observed-law functional follows from consistency and event transport; it is not imposed by definition.\n\nFix a successful pair \\((s,k)\\), abbreviate \\(\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\) by \\(\\bar B_s\\), and fix \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb R^{q_r}\\).  The event part of the criterion in \\texttt{def:fwl-regression} satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac1{n_s}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}(B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}-\\theta-e_0^{\\top}\\gamma)^2\n=\\frac1{n_s}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}(B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}-\\bar B_s)^2\n+(\\bar B_s-\\theta-e_0^{\\top}\\gamma)^2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\gamma\\), the unique minimizing event coefficient is consequently\n\\[\n\\widehat\\theta_{sk}=\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}-e_0^{\\top}\\gamma.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nAt (5), the event criterion is independent of \\(\\gamma\\), so the profiled criterion, apart from the first constant in (4), is\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}w_{stk}\n\\{Q_{sti}-b_r(u_{stk})^{\\top}\\gamma\\}^2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThis is exactly the historical weighted least-squares problem in \\texttt{def:feasible-estimator}.  Passage of the selected normalized Gram-shape test implies \\(G_{sk}\\succ0\\), hence (6) has the unique selected minimizer \\(\\widehat\\gamma_{sk}\\).  Thus\n\\[\n\\widehat\\theta_{sk}=\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}-e_0^{\\top}\\widehat\\gamma_{sk}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nFinally, using the definitions of \\(\\widehat m_s\\), \\(\\theta_{\\mathrm{FWL}}\\), and \\(\\widehat\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta_{\\mathrm{FWL}}\n&=\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\frac{\\pi_s}{K_D}\\sum_{k=1}^{K_D}\n (\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}-e_0^{\\top}\\widehat\\gamma_{sk})\\\\\n&=\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s(\\bar B_s^{\\mathrm{obs}}-\\widehat m_s)\n=\\widehat\\tau.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis identity is algebraic on every sample on which all selected fits exist.",
